          { name: "Assuming Demographic Destiny Produces Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "The 64% result is a clear victory, but it is not a landslide, and in a district where voters are educated and affluent enough to evaluate on policy rather than partisan identity, a 36% opposition exists for reasons that can be addressed or amplified. Any incumbent who treats this margin as demographically inevitable and stops engaging the college-educated professional and younger renter constituencies on their specific concerns will find that margin compressed in the next election; a challenger who articulates a coherent alternative on fiscal policy or cultural issues can plausibly peel away 5-7 percentage points from this coalition." },
          { name: "Treating Hispanic and Latino Constituents as Part of the Democratic Coalition Rather Than an Engaged Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 16.69%, the Hispanic and Latino population in CO-HD-23 is substantial and clearly voting Democratic—but the 64% overall margin does not reveal whether these constituents are being actively engaged on their distinct economic priorities (wages, housing, healthcare access) or simply absorbed into a broader coalition narrative. Neglecting specific outreach and legislative attention to this constituency's material concerns risks both losing mobilization in a close election and failing to deliver on the governance obligations that represent them." }
