          { name: "Ideological Polarization in Campaign Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "CO-HD-27's Democratic voters are not animated by ideological progressivism but by perceptions of competence and professional economic interest. A Democratic candidate who campaigns on cultural or ideological distinctiveness rather than on school funding, property tax management, and local service quality is misreading the district and will activate the 43% Republican opposition around 'ideology vs. pragmatism' rather than allowing the election to turn on the professional economic questions where Democrats have current advantage. The realignment is fragile if treated as ideological rather than as performance-based." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 43% Opposition as Permanently Republican", tag: "avoid", why: "The 43% opposition in a $129K-income, 57% college-educated suburb is not a frozen Republican base but a constituency of affluent professionals who voted differently than their demographic profile might predict — meaning they are capable of evaluating candidates and issues differently than inherited partisan identity suggests. Any candidate who treats the 43% as immovable Republicans rather than as persuadable professionals whose current opposition reflects specific policy grievances (school funding, local governance, tax burden) will miss the opportunity to compete for swing voters in a genuinely competitive district. The margin is real but narrow, and the opposition is not ideologically locked." }
