          "The 61% margin is durable but not immune to shift—particularly if Democratic messaging becomes abstract or disconnected from the material concerns of the working-class and minority households that comprise the district's core. Hispanic voters in this district, at 26.21%, have demonstrated capacity to shift based on specific messaging around economic mobility, immigration enforcement approaches, and whether candidates address the cost of living directly. Black voters, at 18.02%, are evaluating on education quality, criminal justice outcomes, and economic opportunity in ways that demand specific legislative answers, not generic appeals to community. White college-educated voters (likely overrepresented in the 33.6% college-attainment figure) are in this coalition as well, and their support for Democratic candidates depends on continued alignment on social issues and climate policy—but also increasingly on whether Democratic governance delivers on inflation, housing costs, and service delivery. Any challenger mounting a credible campaign in this district will target the 39% who currently oppose—likely by nationalizing the race around cost-of-living issues, immigration, or public safety—and by attempting to peel off 3-4 percentage points from one of the three largest demographic communities. The incumbent's challenge is to consolidate the existing coalition through active constituent engagement, to address the material concerns that animate each community's vote, and to maintain visibility on whether government is actually improving people's economic circumstances.",
