          "Lead with property values, school funding, and tax efficiency: the 72.2% white, 79.4% homeowner-majority constituency is voting on whether the district's public goods — schools, infrastructure, service quality — justify their tax burden. Specific commitments to school funding adequacy, property tax management, and local infrastructure investment are far more electorally potent in CO-SD-5 than abstract fiscal conservatism.",
          "Engage Hispanic constituents as economic peers, not as a minority interest: the 22.45% Hispanic population in an affluent suburban district includes business owners, homeowners, and dual-income professionals with direct stakes in school quality, property values, and economic opportunity. Campaign messaging and governance priorities that treat Hispanic families as fellow stakeholders in the district's economic prosperity — not as a demographic to be managed — will perform better than immigration-focused framing in a district where Hispanic and white economic interests substantially overlap.",
