          "The fact that Exum won with exactly 50% in 2022 — in a cycle where his party held the governorship and statewide momentum — suggests that the district's baseline is competitive and volatile rather than safely held. A 50% result is a plurality, not a mandate, and the 50% opposition includes voters actively dissatisfied with the current representation or genuinely persuadable by an alternative. The combination of young age (31.5 median), economic constraint ($63,007 income with 47.2% renters), and multiethnic composition means this district's voters are pragmatic rather than tribal — they will reward a representative who delivers concrete improvements in housing affordability, healthcare access, or job opportunities, and will punish one who delivers abstractions or fails to address the economic pressures they face daily. Any candidate seeking to win or hold this seat must treat the 50% opposition as the primary strategic fact and offer specific, material answers to why their approach will improve the economic security of renters, young families, and workers of color in ways the current representative has not."
